  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "R2 already applies four-hour high-energy ball milling followed by ten-minute plasma sintering to the closely related CoCrFeNi system, while R3 demonstrates essentially the same powder-processing route in an equiatomic Cr-Fe-Ni-containing HEA and connects fine grains and solution strengthening to high hardness. R9 supplies the particularly relevant teaching that Ti addition to CoCrFeNi-derived alloys promotes harder BCC-containing structures, and R15 independently links grain refinement and an FCC/BCC microstructure in an Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy to substantially increased strength. Together, these records would plausibly lead an expert to replace Nb or Al with Ti, vary milling duration and SPS temperature, and seek a dense nanocrystalline FCC/BCC CoCrFeNiTi alloy strengthened by grain boundaries, milling-generated defects, and solid-solution/phase effects. The exact 83/17 phase fraction and reported property values would still require experiment, but the intervention and core mechanism are reconstructible."
}
